>>
>> -- EXPERT in their weapon,
>> -- wielding a weapon they normally get BONUSES with,
>> -- MAXED OUT on his luck,
>> -- and using a MAGIC weapon,
>>
>> can't even get a lousy 1 in 3 chance of hitting a piddly AC -8 monster?
> 
> Very few monsters are below -8 AC.

Missing the point entirely.  The player is alone against the world.  He
is SUPPOSED to have advantages.  When you render disenchanters
nigh-untouchable, there's a problem.

(Also, trying to balance a formula like this from a simple grep against
monst.c leaves out the possibility of those monsters which can wear
armor doing so, something which can certainly throw a wrench in.)

> A level 14 monk against Master Kaen would go something like:
> AC -10 * 5 = -50
> + level 14 * Grandmaster (5) = 20
> + 13 luck = 33
> + 1 for ST 18 * 5 = 38
> + 4 for DX 18 * 5 = 58
> 
> So a better than even chance of hitting.

Also garbage.  The above (snipped) example for the Master Assassin was
better; a 95% chance to hit is more appropriate for a player.  But for a
world-class uber-lucky monk to have only a 58% chance of hitting the
nastiest QN in the game?  Sorry, fails the common sense test again.

Remember, the player is _supposed_ to win.

> Note that it does require a little bit of training before taking on a
> big nasty, but this will at least soak up skill slots.

A game where you have to be at or near the max level to win isn't a very
well designed game, nor is a game where the level you're "supposed" to
go on the quest and win is actually many levels too short of the mark.

Right now the game is balanced such that an XL14 character can ascend,
and an XL30 character will (typically) find it a cakewalk -- but it
takes time and effort to get to XL30.  The prevalence of (not to mention
ease of obtaining) wraith corpses and gain level potions means that the
time and effort required isn't remotely as much as it should be ideally,
but the base design is still actually valid.

Rather than spending your time finding ways to screw over lower-level
players, you should be spending more time playing the game, so you
understand how this all works in the first place.

